<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basil of Caesarea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/330">330</a>–379</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Saint Basil the Great, Basil the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basil of Caesarea, also called Saint Basil the Great, was an early Christian prelate who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Caesarea Mazaca</a> in Cappadocia from 370 until his death in 379. An influential theologian, he supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a> and opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> and Apollinarianism. He founded the Basileias before the gates of Caesarea, a poorhouse, hospital and hospice believed to be one of the earliest Christian hospitals for the poor, and set guidelines for monastic life built on community life, liturgical prayer and manual labor. With his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-nyssa">Gregory of Nyssa</a> and his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-nazianzus">Gregory of Nazianzus</a> he is counted among the Cappadocian Fathers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basil-of-caesarea.jpg" alt="Basil of Caesarea" width="330" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basil_of_Caesarea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the time of <b>Basil of Caesarea</b> (329–379) it was already old: he spoke of its singing as a cherished tradition of the church, though some attribute the hymn to Basil himself.</blockquote><a href="/songs/phos-hilaron">Phos Hilaron (O Gladsome Light)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For just as bees know how to extract honey from flowers, which to men are agreeable only for their fragrance and color, even so here also those who look for something more than pleasure and enjoyment in such writers may derive profit for their souls.&#8221;</p><footer>Address to Young Men on Greek Literature, 4 &#183; tr. Frederick Morgan Padelford, 1902 &#183; <a href="https://www.tertullian.org/fathers/basil_litterature01.htm" rel="noopener">www.tertullian.org</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Therefore the soul must be guarded with great care, lest through our love for letters it receive some contamination unawares, as men drink in poison with honey.&#8221;</p><footer>Address to Young Men on Greek Literature, 4 &#183; tr. Frederick Morgan Padelford, 1902 &#183; <a href="https://www.tertullian.org/fathers/basil_litterature01.htm" rel="noopener">www.tertullian.org</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
